        fp: "A commercial real estate investment company entered into a written contract with an architectural design firm to prepare structural renovation blueprints for an office building for $60,000, payable upon completion on August 1. The contract contained the following clause: 'Time is of the essence. Completion and delivery of the final blueprints by August 1 is an express condition precedent to Owner's obligation to pay.' On July 20, the design firm telephoned the investment company's managing director and stated that due to staff turnover, the blueprints would be delivered on August 8. The managing director replied: 'That is completely fine; August 8 will work with our contractor's bidding schedule.' Relying on this conversation, the design firm instructed its draftspersons to work at standard non-overtime billing rates, avoiding a $4,000 rush-overtime payroll expenditure. On August 8, the design firm tendered the fully conforming blueprints. The investment company accepted the blueprints, but refused to pay the $60,000 fee, asserting the failure of the express condition precedent of delivery by August 1.",
